# The two halves of every claim's uncertainty, read together. One row per combination of what
# would make a range smaller and who owns the edge it would have to move, counted over the
# corpus.
#
# One instrument, two tables
#   `narrowsWhen` and `boundOrigin` are both required and typed on `Claim` precisely because they
#   are one instrument, and this is the pair the schema was restructured to make askable. The
#   ingest aligns them on the claim they share, and two censuses never show the crossing. This is
#   the relation that joins them.
#
# Read the largest cell first
#   It is the one that should smart. "Nobody has said what would narrow this" crossed with
#   "nothing sets this bound" is a claim with no course of action and no owner: a bare number pair
#   wearing a three-point estimate's clothes. Neither census alone can see it: in one it is an
#   unremarkable `unmeasured`, in the other an unremarkable `none`. It is the honest measure of
#   how much of this corpus is decoration.
#
# The small cells hold the advice
#   `instrument` against `contractual` says measure it and then go renegotiate; `intervention`
#   against `intrinsic` says the bound does not move at all, so the only way out is to stop being
#   bound by that thing, which is exactly what the row filing it proposes, in prose.
#
# Corpus only
#   The fixtures file combinations on purpose to prove the branches work, and counting a
#   stipulation as evidence of what people actually write is the error this whole scope/
#   directory exists to prevent.
-- epistemics/widths.sqlc against epistemics/edges.sqlc, joined on the claim they share.
SELECT w.what_the_width_is_made_of AS what_would_narrow_it,
       e.who_owns_the_edge         AS who_owns_the_edge,
       count(*)                    AS claims
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/widths.sqlc)
) w
JOIN      (
    :compose(epistemics/edges.sqlc)
) e USING (filing, seq)
JOIN      (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1, 2
ORDER BY 3 DESC, 1, 2
